            <ArticleTitle>Differences in serum sex hormone and plasma lipid levels in Caucasian and African-American premenopausal women.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>4516-20</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="CONTEXT" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Risk of coronary heart disease is higher in African-American than in Caucasian women.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">The aim of this study was to evaluate the contribution of sex hormone levels, race, and measures of body fat to the variation in plasma lipid levels, a well-established risk factor for coronary heart disease.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">This was a cross-sectional study.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">The study was conducted in the general community.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="STUDY PARTICIPANTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Sixty Caucasian and 117 African-American premenopausal women participated.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MAIN OUTCOME MEASURES" NlmCategory="METHODS">Body weight, body mass index (BMI), and waist to hip circumference ratio (WHR), as well as plasma lipid and serum sex hormone levels, were assessed.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Relative to Caucasian women, African-American women had significantly higher mean BMI (23.92 +/- 3.87 vs. 26.99 +/- 5.87 kg/m2, respectively; P &lt; 0.001), and WHR (0.733 +/- 0.052 vs. 0.757 +/- 0.068; P &lt; 0.03). Also, plasma triglyceride (TG) levels were significantly lower in African-American women (81 +/- 61 vs. 55 +/- 24 mg/dl; P &lt; 0.0001). Serum estrone sulfate (556 +/- 323 vs. 442 +/- 332 pg/ml, Caucasian vs. African-American; P &lt; 0.001), estradiol (E2) (55.1 +/- 43.6 vs. 35.8 +/- 17.7 pg/ml; P &lt; 0.0001), androstenedione (2.6 +/- 0.9 vs. 1.6 +/- 0.7 ng/ml; P &lt; 0.0001), and testosterone (0.36 +/- 0.12 vs. 0.31 +/- 0.19 ng/ml; P &lt; 0.002) levels were significantly lower in African-American women than in Caucasian women. After correction for the effects of age, BMI, and WHR, serum E2 levels were significantly and positively associated with plasma high-density lipoprotein cholesterol levels in all women, and serum estrone sulfate levels with plasma total cholesterol and TG levels in African-American women.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Our results indicate that race is an important determinant of plasma TG and serum sex hormone levels, even after adjustment for differences in body size. A significant association between endogenous E2 and high-density lipoprotein cholesterol levels exists in premenopausal women, independent of their race.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
